Wait I already have B4. So B2,B3,B4 Chow already complete in hand (B2,B3,B4) \u2014 that\u2019s 3 tiles forming a Chow!  \nYes: B2,B3,B4 is a Chow. So that's second concealed Chow. So hand structure:  \nMeld 1: [W2,W3,W4] (exposed).  \nMeld 2: [B2,B3,B4] (concealed).  \nRemaining: W4, T4, T5, B6, B7, F1\u00d72, F3 (7 tiles).  \n\nFrom these:  \nB6,B7 could combine with B5 or B8 for Chow.  \nT4,T5 could combine with T3 or T6 for Chow, but T3 exhausted, T6 only 1 left.  \nF1\u00d72 is a pair candidate.  \nW4 is singleton but could pair with my exposed meld's W4? No, meld uses W4 already, but I have extra W4 so possibly pair with W4 if I get another W4 (W4: 1 left in wall).  \nF3 singleton.  \n\nSo to progress toward 4 melds + pair:  \nOption A: Keep B6,B7, try to draw B5 or B8.  \nOption B: Keep T4,T5, try to draw T6 (rare) or T3 (gone) \u2014 not promising. So maybe discard T4 or T5.  \nOption C: Keep F1 pair, discard one of F1 to go for pung? No, keep pair for final pair.  \n\nBest immediate discard among valid actions: likely T4 or T5 (dead sequence because T3 gone, T6 scarce). Or F3 (useless). Or B6/B7 if I don't think B5/B8 draw likely (B5: 2 left, B8: 3 left \u2014 not too bad).  \n\nAlso check scoring patterns:  \nSeat wind is North Wind (F4), prevalent wind South Wind (F2). I have none. F1 pung possible if I get one more F1 (4 left). Could get Pung of Terminals or Honors (F1 counts).  \n\n**Promising discards:**  \n1. **Discard T4** \u2014 removes a tile in a weak suit sequence with few outs.  \n2. **Discard T5** \u2014 same reasoning, but T5 has 4 left, could be useful if I draw T6 and T7? Unlikely because sequence T4,T5,T6 would need T4 back.  \n3. **Discard F3** \u2014 no sequence possible, only honor singleton, but F3 has 2 left, could pair and maybe get F3 pung. But less useful than F1.  \n4. **Discard B6** \u2014 breaks B6,B7 Chow chance, but B5,B6,B7 possible if I have B5 (don't have), or B6,B7,B8 possible if draw B8. B8 has 3 left, so decent chance. So