ws**: Aim for four chows + pair. I already have two chows. Could try to make chow from B6,B7 (need B5 or B8), and maybe chow from W1,W4? Not consecutive. Or chow from honors? No.  \n3. **All Types**: Need dragon, don't have.  \n\nGiven the concealed B chow, keeping it might help with \"Concealed Hand\" points later. Also, having two F1 could become a pung for points (Pung of Honors, 1 point each).  \n\nDiscard candidates:  \n- T5: Isolated, no other T, less useful.  \n- B6 or B7: Might break potential B chow sequence, but B6,B7 can pair with B5/B8 to form chow; but B5 has 2 left, B8 has 3 left, possible.  \n- W1: Terminal, might be useful for outside hand? But I have W4 also.  \n- W4: I have only one, and W4 is simple, not terminal.  \n- F3: Single honor, no pair.  \n\nI think discarding T5 is reasonable: it's the only tile of its suit, not contributing to sequences or pairs. Discarding it keeps flexibility with B and W suits and honors.  \n\nAlternative: discard B2? But B2 is part of the concealed chow B2,B3,B4; discarding B2 would break that chow, which is a completed meld. That would be bad. So not discard B2,B3,B4.  \n\nSo discard among: T5, B6, B7, W1, W4, F3.  \nI want to keep the F1 pair for potential pung.  \nMaybe discard W1 because it's terminal and I don't have another W1 (3 left though, could draw), but W1 is terminal, if I aim for outside hand, terminals are good. Outside hand requires every meld and pair contain terminal or honor. Currently, my two chows (W234 and B234) have no terminals or honors (W2,W3,W4 are simples, B2,B3,B4 are simples). So for outside hand, I'd need to change melds. That seems unlikely.  \n\nGiven the situation, discarding T5 seems safest, reducing suit clutter.  \n\nReason: T5 is an isolated tile from a suit where I have no other tiles, making it the least useful for forming chows or pungs. Discarding it preserves my concealed chow (B2,B3,B4) and keeps options open for forming melds from W tiles or honors, while maintaining the pair of F1. This move maintains hand flexibility and doesn't break existing potential melds.\n\nAnswer: Play T5"}
